 - **Availability first.** Rate limiting sits on the critical path of every call. If the counter store is unreachable, the default is to allow (fail open) and log loudly, rather than convert a limiter blip into a full gateway outage. Authorization continues to fail closed independently.
 
-## The Two Axes and Windows
+## The Three Axes and Windows
 
-A limit applies to one of two **axes**:
+A limit applies to one of three **axes**:
 
 - **Caller axis (Limit A):** a caller (a user or agent), across *all* targets, may not exceed N requests per window. Limits target a **group** (name = group). A group carries **two separate numbers** — `user_max_requests` (applied to human callers) and `agent_max_requests` (applied to agent/M2M callers); at least one is required. The caller type is derived from the token: a genuine machine (`client_credentials`) token ⇒ agent, otherwise user. The distinction is made from the token's own `client_id` **claim** (present only on `client_credentials` tokens), NOT the `azp`-derived `client_id` that every OIDC token carries — otherwise a human browser/password-grant token (whose `azp` is the web OAuth client) would be misclassified as an agent. The matching number is used; a group that does not set that type's number does not gate that caller. Enforced **per caller** (each caller gets their own quota). If a caller is in several groups with limits at the same window, the **most restrictive** wins.
 
@@ -182,6 +182,7 @@ A limit applies to one of two **axes**:
   - **Admin bypass.** A caller with the admin role skips caller gates entirely (target gates still apply). An operator cannot rate-limit themselves out of the tool that manages the limits.
   - **Config-time floors.** `RATE_LIMIT_USER_FLOOR_PER_MIN` (default 20) and `RATE_LIMIT_AGENT_FLOOR_PER_MIN` (default 10) are pure config (no API). On short windows (`<= 60s`), a group definition whose user/agent number is below its floor is **rejected at config time**. Longer (hourly/daily volume) windows are exempt so legitimate low volume caps still work.
 - **Target axis (Limit B):** a target *entity*, across *all* callers combined, may not exceed `max_requests` per window. Only enforced for targets that define a limit. This protects a weak backend from combined load, and is **not** bypassed by admin.
+- **Caller-target axis (Limit C, issue #1504):** an independent quota **per (caller, target) pair**. A definition names the **target** (`entity_type` = `mcp_server` | `a2a_agent`, `name` = target path), the rate-limit **`group`** the caller must belong to, and the usual per-caller-type numbers (`user_max_requests` / `agent_max_requests`, at least one). The counter subject is the composite `<identity>|<target_name>`, so one noisy tenant can never consume another tenant's headroom against the same server, and a caller's traffic to server X never draws from its traffic to server Y. The definition id is five-segment: `caller_target:<entity_type>:<group>:<name>:<window_seconds>`. The config-time floors apply to this axis exactly as to the caller axis, and it is enforced at the same `/validate` hook with the same deny-does-not-consume behavior.
 
 The target axis is **entity-type-generic**. v1 enforces two target kinds:
 
@@ -190,7 +191,24 @@ The target axis is **entity-type-generic**. v1 enforces two target kinds:
 
 The model also reserves `mcp_tool` and `a2a_skill` (name = `<parent>:<leaf>`) for a later phase; those require reading the JSON-RPC payload and are not enforced in v1 (the admin API rejects them with a clear message so an operator never gets a silently inert limit).
 
-**Windows and volume limits.** A window is any length from one second up to a full day (`window_seconds` ≤ 86400). A per-day volume cap ("no more than 5000 calls/day") is therefore the *same mechanism* as a per-second burst cap, just a longer window. A single subject may hold **several limits at different windows at once** — e.g. `100/min` *and* `5000/day`. Each window is enforced as its own independent gate, and all applicable gates must pass. Most-restrictive resolution applies only *within* the same window.
+**Windows and volume limits.** A window is any length from one second up to a full day (`window_seconds` <= 86400). A per-day volume cap ("no more than 5000 calls/day") is therefore the *same mechanism* as a per-second burst cap, just a longer window. A single subject may hold **several limits at different windows at once** — e.g. `100/min` *and* `5000/day`. Each window is enforced as its own independent gate, and all applicable gates must pass. Most-restrictive resolution applies only *within* the same window.
+
+## Quarantine (Kill Switch)
+
+Issue #1504 adds two **reserved rate-limit groups** that act as an absolute deny, evaluated BEFORE any gate/counter work at `/validate`:
+
+- `__quarantined_callers__` — add this group to a caller's rate-limit membership (Users/M2M pages, membership API, or `registry_management.py rate-limit-quarantine-caller --subject <user|client>`) and every data-plane request from that caller is dropped.
+- `__quarantined_targets__` — a `target`-subject membership carrying this group (Rate Limits page quarantine panel, or `rate-limit-quarantine-target --subject <path>`) pulls that target out of rotation for EVERY caller.
+
+Both groups are **seeded automatically** (as frozen anchor documents in `rate_limit_memberships`) at auth-server startup, are undeletable (DELETE returns 409), and can never appear in a `RateLimitDefinition` (PUT returns 400): quarantine is a kill switch, never a throttle.
+
+Decided semantics:
+
+- **Response shape.** A throttle leaves `/validate` as a 403 carrying `X-RateLimit-Throttled: 1` and `Retry-After` (nginx rewrites it to 429). A quarantine deny carries `X-RateLimit-Quarantined: 1` and **no** `Retry-After`, and is NOT rewritten: a kill switch is indefinite, so the response must not promise (or reveal) a clear time. The body `error` is `caller_quarantined` / `target_quarantined` rather than `rate_limit_exceeded`.
+- **Admin bypass follows the axis.** An admin caller bypasses its own caller quarantine (consistent with the caller-gate bypass: an operator must not lock themselves out). A quarantined TARGET denies everyone, admins included (consistent with target gates). Accepted risk: a compromised ADMIN token is not contained by caller quarantine; contain it per target via target quarantine and revoke admin status at the IdP.
+- **Store-unavailable failure mode.** The caller-quarantine check rides the caller-membership lookup that caller gates already perform (no new dependency). The target-quarantine lookup follows the global `RATE_LIMIT_FAIL_OPEN` policy: fail-open (allow + log) by default; deployments that set `RATE_LIMIT_FAIL_OPEN=false` get a fail-closed quarantine.
+- **Observability.** Denies increment `mcpgw_rate_limit_quarantined_total{subject=caller|target}` (bounded labels; the identity is in the WARNING log). An increase in this metric means a kill switch is ACTIVE - page an operator.
+- **Release.** Remove the reserved group from the subject's membership (or `rate-limit-quarantine-caller --subject X --release`). It takes effect on the next request; membership mutations invalidate the read cache.
 
 ## Data Model
 
